+
+# applyPatch semantics
+
+## Context
+Services need to update JSON documents with RFC 6902 patches without surprising side effects. The
+RFC leaves some cases open: whole-document removal, moving a value into its own child, and what
+`test` equality means.
+
+## Decision
+- Pure and atomic: the document is cloned on entry (`structuredClone`) and operations mutate only
+  that copy; values taken from the patch (`add`, `replace`) or the document (`copy`) are cloned on
+  insertion. Any failure throws, so callers never see a partial result.
+- The whole patch is validated before any operation runs: it must be an array of objects with a known
+  lowercase `op`, a valid pointer `path`, `from` for move/copy, and an own `value` member for
+  add/replace/test (an explicit `null` is a value). Unknown members are ignored (RFC appendix A.11).
+- Paths use the strict dl-001 pointer rules. `-` is accepted only as the last token of `add`, to
+  append; array `add` accepts indices `0..length`, all other operations require an existing target.
+- `remove` of the root throws (there is no document left to return). `move` into a proper child of
+  its source throws; `move` onto itself is a no-op once the source is known to exist.
+- `test` compares JSON values structurally: object member order is ignored, array order and types matter.
+- Properties are set with `defineProperty`, so a `__proto__` key stays plain data and never changes a
+  prototype.
+
+## Consequences
+Cost of one deep clone per call, in exchange for safety. Non-JSON values (functions, cycles, etc.)
+are not supported and `structuredClone` may throw on them.
